EXT. CANYON FLOOR - WRECKED HOT AIR BALLOON - DAY

Red rock walls rise a thousand feet. The balloon's envelope lies draped across boulders like a collapsed circus tent, stripes of orange and white fluttering. The basket is on its side, wicker split.

A WOMAN (40s, flight suit, blood on her temple, one boot missing) crawls from the wreckage, dragging a first-aid kit. MARGOT BRANDT-KIM (30s, pilot's vest, sun-hat torn, ropeburn on both palms) sits on a boulder, drinking from a canteen.

                    MARGOT
          Don't stand.

                    WOMAN
          I'm fine.

                    MARGOT
          You're not fine. You're bleeding.

                    WOMAN
          So are you.

Margot looks at her hands. Shrugs. Passes the canteen across.

                    MARGOT
          I'm Margot.

                    WOMAN
          Soledad.

                    MARGOT
          Soledad. Nice to meet you. Sorry about the crash.

                    SOLEDAD
          It wasn't your fault.

                    MARGOT
          I'm the pilot. It's always my fault.

Soledad drinks. Wipes her mouth. Gives the canteen back. They sit, shoulder to shoulder, small figures at the bottom of a very large place.

                    SOLEDAD
          How far to the road?

                    MARGOT
          Twelve miles. Maybe fifteen.

                    SOLEDAD
          We have two liters.

                    MARGOT
          We have one and a half.

She gestures at the canteen. A thin trickle leaks from the base. Soledad closes her eyes.

                    SOLEDAD
          So we leave now.

                    MARGOT
          We leave at dusk. She's too hot.

                    SOLEDAD
          Who?

                    MARGOT
          The sun.

Soledad laughs, briefly, then winces. Her hand goes to her ribs.

                    SOLEDAD
          Margot, you should know something.

                    MARGOT
          Okay.

                    SOLEDAD
          I didn't buy a ticket. I stowed away.

Margot looks at her. Slowly puts down the canteen.

                    MARGOT
          Why?

                    SOLEDAD
          Because she told me you'd stop if I asked.

                    MARGOT
          Who told you?

                    SOLEDAD
          Your sister.

Margot doesn't move. A hawk circles overhead, a dark scribble on the pale sky.

                    MARGOT
          My sister is dead.

                    SOLEDAD
          Yes. She was very clear about that.

Margot stands. Soledad lifts a hand, palm out.

                    SOLEDAD (CONT'D)
          Sit. Please. We have twelve miles to talk.

                    MARGOT
          I'm going to kill you.

                    SOLEDAD
          Not before dusk.
